<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="e5c24272">&lt;p&gt;
    If the client has a mature enterprise architecture practice, leverage the logical models related to BDAT
    (business, data, application, technology) domains to support developing the logical scope sections
    in the charter.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    As the project progresses it is critical that either the project charter itself is updated to stay current or that it
    points to the most current versions of any summarized artifacts.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="e465dd5b"></attribute>
<attribute name="usageGuidance" nodeId="30751772"></attribute>
<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="3ce1bbba">&lt;p&gt;
    The project charter defines a discrete unit of work that can be independently staffed and managed. The charter
    specifies the scope of the project and its completion criteria. It provides an agreement of what the project is
    committed to deliver, the budget, time constraints, resources, and standards within which it must be completed. The
    project charter specifies the boundary of the project – what is included and what is excluded. It also defines the
    required project management and control structure. The charter may be changed during the life of the project. At any
    time, however, it represents the agreed commitment to the parameters it defines.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The project charter must always represent the current perception of the project boundaries. The charter is used as the
    foundation for preparing detailed plans to be used throughout the life of the project. It also serves as a statement of
    the commitment of an organization to a particular project. For all these reasons, the integrity of the project charter
    must be preserved throughout a project. This does not mean that the charter will not or should not change; in fact,
    very few projects are completed without some alterations to their charters. The key point to remember is that the
    project must be kept on a course consistent with the boundaries specified in the charter. Any deviations from that
    course must be considered carefully, documented, and approved by the project sponsor through the change management
    process that has been established for the project.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
